23:05:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That snow-roof analogy is the perfect way to highlight the key difference, because it gets at exactly why our everyday intuition about “weight piling up” can be misleading. The crucial distinction is between **bending** and **compression**, and the role of **lateral bracing**. ### A roof under snow is a horizontal, unsupported span A roof (or a beam, or a shelf) is designed to carry a load *across an empty space*. The weight of the snow pushes down on the flat surface, and the roof has to resist that by bending. Wood, stone, or even steel can only bend so far before the fibers on one side snap or the material buckles. There’s nothing underneath the roof to hold it up except its own stiffness across the gap. When the snow gets too heavy, the bending stress overcomes the material’s strength, and it collapses downward. That’s a classic *flexural failure* — the load is perpendicular to the surface, and the structure is unsupported from below. That’s what you picture with a roof caving in. ### A buried wall is in a completely different situation Now imagine a wall standing on the ground, and you carefully backfill both sides with loose sand until the entire room is solidly packed, level with the top of the wall. Then you add more sand on top, forming a deep layer. What happens to the wall? - The wall is vertical, not horizontal. The weight of the sand above it is *not* a load pushing it sideways; it’s pushing straight down, in the same direction as the wall’s natural strength. Bricks and stone are extraordinarily strong in compression — a single modern brick can support a few tons, and even ancient mud bricks can take considerable vertical load. - Critically, the sand on both sides provides **lateral bracing**. A wall can fail under vertical compression if it buckles sideways, like a slender column. But when it’s packed in by soil on both faces, it *can’t* buckle. It’s essentially a thin, stiff layer inside a larger granular block. - Most importantly, the weight of the overlying soil is **not resting solely on the top edge of the wall**. The soil is a granular mass that distributes its own weight through countless grain-to-grain contacts. The wall is just an inclusion. The vertical stress at the depth of the wall top is the weight of the column of soil above, but that stress exists throughout the entire horizontal cross-section of the fill, not just on the wall. The wall itself experiences that same vertical pressure, but it’s perfectly able to carry it because it’s in pure compression and laterally restrained. Think of a coin buried upright in a jar of sand. If you press down on the top of the sand column with a piston, the coin doesn’t get crushed flat. The sand transmits the force through its own structure; the coin simply feels a high but uniform pressure from all sides. It might get pressed deeper into the sand, but it won’t bend or buckle because there’s no empty space for it to bend into. A wall in a completely filled room is analogous: it becomes part of a solid medium, not a freestanding structure bearing a heavy roof. ### The crushing you imagine comes from a different mechanism You’re right that *some* ruins look like they’ve been crushed, and sometimes they have. That happens when: - An empty room is suddenly buried by a thick, heavy layer, and the walls are pushed inwards from one side only (like a retaining wall failing). That’s a lateral load, not a slow, even burial. - The upper part of a wall was left standing above the fill for centuries, exposed, and eventually toppled from erosion, adding rubble on top. - The building was already ruinous before burial, and the rubble was simply covered and preserved. But the intact brick structures that archaeologists excavate — like the multistory mud-brick houses at Çatalhöyük or the Harappan warehouses — are proof that slow burial did not crush them
