23:05:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I appreciate you bringing in your firsthand experience—that’s a really valuable perspective, and it deserves a careful answer. What you’re describing absolutely happens, and I think the guides may have inadvertently oversimplified the cause. Let me try to bridge what you saw with what I’ve been explaining, because both are true in different contexts. You’re right that at many sites, the walls buried for the longest time (often the deepest and earliest levels) look more crumbled and cracked. But the culprit is almost never *the sheer static weight* of the soil above them gradually squeezing them to pieces, for the engineering reasons I mentioned. Instead, the extra damage comes from other factors that *correlate* with being buried deeper and longer. ### 1. The “buried longer” layers are often in a much wetter environment The deeper you go, the closer you get to the water table. Walls that spent millennia in damp, anaerobic conditions can undergo severe chemical and physical decay. Mud bricks can rehydrate and turn back into soft clay, which then slumps and cracks. Even fired bricks can be attacked by salts dissolved in groundwater. When the water table fluctuates, it causes cycles of swelling and shrinking, which slowly turns bricks to powder. This isn’t “crushing by weight”; it’s water damage. Many of the most crumbled walls at deeply buried sites were essentially rotting away chemically long before any archaeologist touched them. ### 2. Differential settlement, not vertical crushing When a site is buried under many meters of soil, the entire mound compresses over time. If the underlying soil is uneven—say, one section of the wall sits on bedrock and another on soft fill—the wall will settle unevenly, causing shear cracks and tilting. Again, it’s not the top-down weight crushing the bricks, but the ground beneath shifting. The wall is being slowly bent or torn apart by that uneven foundation movement, which becomes more pronounced the deeper the wall is. ### 3. Earlier structures were built differently and already in decay The deepest walls are usually the oldest ones. They might have been built with poorer-quality bricks, thinner foundations, or already half-ruined when they were built over. Later inhabitants often razed and levelled earlier structures before building on top, sometimes leaving only the most damaged footings. So the damage you see might predate the burial entirely. The rubble you see wasn’t created by the weight of centuries of dirt; it was already there when the dirt started accumulating. ### 4. The “burial as protection” effect has limits: it works optimally in dry, desert conditions The perfect preservation of intact brick structures (like those in Egypt or the hyper-arid parts of the Indus Valley) depends on the fill staying bone-dry and chemically inert. At many sites with seasonal rain or high groundwater, burial actually accelerates decay through moisture and salt action, even if it protects against wind and sun. That’s why guides at, say, a European Roman site often point out that the deepest walls are the most damaged: they’ve been sitting in damp, chemically aggressive soil for two millennia. But it’s the damp, not the weight, doing the damage. ### Reconciling your experience with the intact cities I mentioned The glorious intact brick structures that make headlines—like the Harappan granaries or Çatalhöyük’s painted walls—are often exceptions where the burial environment was extraordinarily stable, dry, and gentle. At the same site, you can find a well-preserved wall right next to a completely disintegrated one, because the microenvironment differed by a few centimeters in drainage or soil chemistry. When you tour a ruin, the guide is likely showing you a mixture: some walls collapsed from water damage, some from ancient earthquakes, and some are perfectly intact. When they say “buried longest, most crumbled,” they might be pointing to a real pattern but misattributing it